      "state": {}
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Projection can dramatically change distances and even send a point to infinity, but it preserves the cross-ratio of four ordered collinear points.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a clean white SVG diagram with thick black projection rays radiating from O at the left. Draw three magenta transversals: a near-vertical P line crossing all four rays near the left-middle, a steep downward Q line crossing the same four rays farther right, and a shallow downward R line crossing the upper three rays and running parallel to the lowest ray so R1 is shown by a rightward arrow to infinity. Points should be solid black circles with large serif labels using subscripts. Keep the original visual hierarchy: black for projection rays and points, vivid magenta for the transversal lines. Add a small live cross-ratio panel in an unobtrusive corner, with equal values emphasized when the configuration is valid."
